1
00:00:01,680 --> 00:00:06,960
Starting with this lecture, we are going to 
discuss classic synchronization problems. 

2
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:11,760
The first such problem is the 
bounded-buffer producer/consumer problem.

3
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:17,120
Just a reminder: what we are trying 
to do here is to study/discuss  

4
00:00:17,120 --> 00:00:20,000
how we can use basic building blocks,  

5
00:00:20,640 --> 00:00:26,080
synchronization primitives in this case, to 
compose applications that have certain semantics.

6
00:00:27,680 --> 00:00:31,200
So, for the bounded producer-consumer 
buffer, it looks like this. 

7
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:37,680
We have one producer that wants to add items 
in a queue and a consumer that reads them. 

8
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:42,800
As you can imagine, the queue itself 
is what we need to handle with care,  

9
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:48,480
inside the critical section. Why is that? 
because the producer must be sure there's  

10
00:00:48,480 --> 00:00:52,320
space in the buffer before adding 
an element (it is a bounded buffer),  

11
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:57,840
and the consumer must be sure there's 
something in the buffer before reading it. 

12
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:03,840
Let’s try to find a solution for this 
critical section using semaphores

13
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:10,880
A way to put down such ideas is to draw a 
diagram like the following one. As you can see,  

14
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:15,440
each critical section of a thread is 
"wrapped" in a lock (this is just what  

15
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:22,400
we discussed before). The unlocking of the 
critical section of a thread precedes the locking  

16
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:28,960
of the critical section of any of the two threads.
For the producer, an extra wait should precede  

17
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:34,160
the locking of its critical section, because 
if the queue is full the thread has to wait. 

18
00:01:35,360 --> 00:01:38,960
For the consumer thread, a 
while loop should precede  

19
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:44,400
the locking of its critical section to make 
sure there are actually elements in the queue. 

20
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:52,000
When can the producer exit the while? 
after the consumer removed an element.  

21
00:01:52,560 --> 00:01:57,120
This can be signaled for sure after the 
consumer exits its critical section. 

22
00:01:57,840 --> 00:02:02,720
When can the consumer exit the while? 
after the producer added an element.  

23
00:02:02,720 --> 00:02:07,840
This can be signaled for sure after the 
producer exits its critical section.

24
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:13,120
If you draw such a scheme, is 
then easier to come up with  

25
00:02:13,680 --> 00:02:18,080
pseudocode like the following one. As 
for the previous lecture, take a moment  

26
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:24,720
later on and think about how to formally 
reason about the validity of this solution.

